
# Git Notes

## 1. `git stash`

Use `git stash` when you have **uncommitted changes** in your working directory and you want to temporarily save them without committing.

### Save current changes

```bash
git stash
```

* Saves the changes as a backup.
* Removes the changes from the working directory.
* The working directory becomes clean.

### View all stashes

```bash
git stash list
```

Example:

```text
stash@{0}: WIP on main: 3a2b1c4 Added login feature
stash@{1}: WIP on feature: 8f4c2d1 Bug fix
```

### Apply a stash

```bash
git stash apply stash@{0}
```

This restores the changes from the specified stash.

> **Note:** It is recommended that your working directory is clean before applying a stash to avoid merge conflicts.

### Remove all stashes

```bash
git stash clear
```

This permanently deletes all saved stashes.

---

# 2. `git cherry-pick`

`git cherry-pick` is used to copy **a specific commit** from one branch to another.

Syntax:

```bash
git cherry-pick <commit-id>
```

Example:

```bash
git cherry-pick 8f4c2d1
```

Only the changes from commit `8f4c2d1` are copied to the current branch.

> **Note:** Cherry-pick works only with committed changes. It cannot copy uncommitted changes.

---

# 3. Undo Working Directory Changes

To discard changes made to a file in the working directory:

```bash
git restore <file-name>
```

Example:

```bash
git restore file1
```

This restores the file to the latest committed version.

---

# 4. View Commit History

To view the last two commits:

```bash
git log --oneline -2
```

Example:

```text
8f4c2d1 Fix login bug
3a2b1c4 Added login page
```

`HEAD` always points to the latest commit on the current branch.

---

# 5. `git reset`

`git reset` moves the `HEAD` pointer to another commit.

## Soft Reset

```bash
git reset --soft HEAD~1
```

* Moves the repository (`HEAD`) to the previous commit.
* Keeps all changes in the **staging area**.
* Working directory remains unchanged.

---

## Mixed Reset (Default)

```bash
git reset --mixed HEAD~1
```

or

```bash
git reset HEAD~1
```

* Moves the repository (`HEAD`) to the previous commit.
* Unstages the changes.
* Keeps the changes in the working directory.

---

## Hard Reset

```bash
git reset --hard HEAD~1
```

* Moves the repository (`HEAD`) to the previous commit.
* Clears the staging area.
* Removes all changes from the working directory.

> **Warning:** `git reset --hard` permanently deletes uncommitted changes.

---

# Difference Between Reset Types

| Command             | Repository (HEAD) | Staging Area | Working Directory |
| ------------------- | ----------------- | ------------ | ----------------- |
| `git reset --soft`  | Reset             | Kept         | Kept              |
| `git reset --mixed` | Reset             | Cleared      | Kept              |
| `git reset --hard`  | Reset             | Cleared      | Cleared           |

---

# 6. `git revert`

`git revert` creates a new commit that reverses the changes introduced by an existing commit.

Syntax:

```bash
git revert <commit-id>
```

Example:

```bash
git revert 8f4c2d1
```

This does **not** delete the original commit. Instead, Git creates a new commit that undoes its changes.

Example:

Before:

```text
A ---- B ---- C (HEAD)
```

After:

```text
A ---- B ---- C ---- D (HEAD)
```

* `C` = Original commit
* `D` = Revert commit that undoes the changes made in `C`

`git revert` is the preferred way to undo changes that have already been pushed to a shared repository because it preserves the commit history.


