# 5. Git Reset

## Soft Reset

```bash
git reset --soft HEAD~1
```

Moves the branch pointer (`HEAD`) back one commit.

* Repository (HEAD) → Reset
* Staging Area → Kept
* Working Directory → Kept

**Use Case**

Undo the last commit but keep all changes staged so that you can recommit them.

---

## Mixed Reset (Default)

```bash
git reset --mixed HEAD~1
```

or

```bash
git reset HEAD~1
```

* Repository (HEAD) → Reset
* Staging Area → Cleared
* Working Directory → Kept

**Use Case**

Undo the commit and keep the changes for further editing.

---

## Hard Reset

```bash
git reset --hard HEAD~1
```

* Repository (HEAD) → Reset
* Staging Area → Cleared
* Working Directory → Cleared

⚠ **Warning:** This permanently deletes uncommitted changes.

---

# Reset Comparison

| Command             | Repository | Staging Area | Working Directory |
| ------------------- | ---------- | ------------ | ----------------- |
| `git reset --soft`  | Reset      | Kept         | Kept              |
| `git reset --mixed` | Reset      | Cleared      | Kept              |
| `git reset --hard`  | Reset      | Cleared      | Cleared           |

